{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable (positive) outcomes compared to unfavorable (zero) outcomes. A lottery is considered left-skewed if more probability is on the favorable side (i.e., higher payoff) than on the unfavorable side (zero). In this case, both lotteries have a zero outcome with high probability (67% for A, 66% for B), and a positive outcome (2500 or 2400 Euros) with the remaining probability. The favorable outcome (non-zero) is thus assigned to the lower probability side: 33% for A and 34% for B. Since both lotteries have a positive outcome only in the smaller probability portion, and the favorable outcome is not concentrated in a larger share of the probability mass, neither lottery is clearly left-skewed. In fact, both are symmetrically skewed toward zero. The reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery — meaning one with a higher probability mass on the positive outcome — but here, both have only 33% and 34% on the positive side, which is small and similar. Thus, the information to assess skewness is present and relevant.",
  "step_2": "The individual notes that in both lotteries, the most likely outcome is zero (no gain or loss), which is consistent with the high probabilities of zero (67% for A, 66% for B). The individual observes that the chance of winning differs only slightly: 33% vs. 34%, with B having a marginally higher probability. The individual concludes that the lotteries are 'in etwa gleich gut' (roughly equal in quality) and that the decision was made spontaneously. There is no mention of skewness, probability distribution shape, or comparison of the probability mass on the positive outcome. The individual does not analyze whether one lottery has more probability mass on the favorable side (i.e., the non-zero outcome). Instead, the focus is on the small difference in winning probability and the similarity of outcomes. The justification is based on similarity and spontaneity, not on any formal rule like skewness assessment.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual does not evaluate whether one lottery has a greater portion of probability on the favorable outcome — which is the core of the skewness criterion. The individual only notes the small difference in winning probabilities and concludes the lotteries are similar. The justification does not reference skewness or any rule about left-skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual's reasoning is based on similarity and spontaneity, not on a formal evaluation of skewness. There is no indication that the individual applied the rule that prefers the more left-skewed lottery. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
